## What the October 2026 Fed Hold Means for Savings, CDs and Mortgages

The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) held the federal funds target range at 3-3/4 to 4 percent on October 28, 2026, and that hold changes no rate you already have. The [Federal Reserve's statement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm) reports a 10 to 2 vote, with Lisa D. Cook and Beth M. Hammack dissenting because they preferred a 1/4 percentage point increase at this meeting. The statement says "Inflation remains elevated" and describes economic activity as expanding at a strong pace, with the unemployment rate little changed.

A certificate of deposit (CD) you already opened keeps its rate for the full term, as the CD section below explains. Each bank sets its own rates on new CDs and savings accounts, so compare current quotes in the [CD calculator](/cd-calculator/) or the [high-yield savings account (HYSA) vs CD comparison](/compare/hysa-vs-cd/) before you pick one. The two dissenting votes are not a decision, and the target range stayed where the September 16 hike put it.

The hold is not a mortgage-rate event, and a fixed-rate loan you already have is unaffected by it. [Freddie Mac's weekly survey](https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms) put the average 30-year fixed rate at 7.24% on October 29, down from 7.32% a week earlier, a move that came with no change in the Fed's target range. Freddie Mac builds that average from rates offered Thursday through Wednesday on loan applications submitted to it, so use the [mortgage rates guide](/guides/current-mortgage-rates-guide/) to compare your own quote against it.

The two dissents show that 2 of the 12 voters wanted a quarter-point increase at this meeting, but the Federal Reserve statement gives no explicit guidance about the next meeting. If you hold bonds or a mortgage rate lock, the sections below explain how a rate change affects each one. Compare current CD and savings quotes before you lock a term, then read the Federal Reserve statement after the [December 8-9, 2026 meeting](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm), which comes with a Summary of Economic Projections.